 Exam material only, **do not publish** (`"private": true` in package.json).
 Distilled from `@huanlin/dsh-plugin-ya-workspace-sidebar` (adaptation commits
 `85f725a` + `a3f317d`); see the task README for provenance.
+
+## Status: browser takeover retired (2026-10-12)
+
+### Original product spec, as the plugin stated it (retired)
+
+> A workspace-browser replacement plugin: the bundle patch disables the
+> official `ui-workspace` client entry and the browser half registers the
+> replacement two-level sidebar (`sidebar.workspaces`) plus the conversation
+> hero picker (`conversation.hero.workspace`), written in the 0.1.1-era style.
+
+The retired entry points and surfaces:
+
+- `sidebar.workspaces` — the two-level sidebar: root navigation with a global
+  Recent Sessions block, then per-workspace session groups.
+- `conversation.hero.workspace` — the hero picker on the empty state.
+- `sidebar.workspaces.directoryFlow` and
+  `conversation.hero.workspace.directoryFlow` — the directory-flow child holes
+  the two registrations declared, plus their `hooks.directoryFlow` occupancy
+  sources.
+- The bundle layer that disabled the official `ui-workspace` client entry so
+  the replacement was the only provider of both seats.
+
+### Why the takeover is gone
+
+dsh 0.1.2-alpha.2 ships those exact entry points officially. The web-app bundle
+mounts `@deepseek-ai/dsh-client-ui-workspace` (`packages/bundle/web-app/cordis.patch.yml`,
+id `ui-workspace`), and its client half registers `sidebar.workspaces` **and**
+`conversation.hero.workspace` and declares both `*.directoryFlow` child holes —
+the same slots this plugin shadowed. The harness's own slot catalog marks the
+two seats `replaceRisk: 'shadows-shipped-ui'`.
+
+The takeover could not coexist with it anyway: both entries register into the
+same `single` slots at the same default priority (`single slot "<name>" already
+has a registration`) and both declare the same child keys (`slot "<child>" is
+already declared`). The old `- id: ui-workspace / disabled: true` row therefore
+removed shipped UI, so it is retired; only the host half is still inserted.
+
+### What survives: session-title projection write-behind
+
+A title provider commits `session/title` after the main request's `turn/end`,
+and a hard host restart never fires `session/disposed`, so the projection
+cache's count/interval throttle can leave the newest title out of the durable
+row (`packages/session/session-projection-cache`). A cold session list then
+falls back to the workspace name even though the title is safe in the log.
+
+`index.js` keeps the write-behind: on `session/title` it asks the cache for a
+checkpoint through the public `sessionProjectionCache.write(session)` seam,
+debounced 2 s and fail-soft. On a profile without the cache service it degrades
+to silence. The 0.1.1-era body wrote title-only rows into the single
+`session_projcache.json` store file; alpha.2 stores one version-stamped
+document per session in a storage domain, so the plugin no longer writes store
+documents itself.
+
+### dsh-side patch
+
+`patches/session-title-projection-flush.patch` is the general-purpose fix kept
+for this capability: treat `session/title` as a mandatory checkpoint point in
+the cache's own write path. The plugin's listener is the fallback for hosts
+that do not carry the patch. See
+`.dsh-migrate/patch-reports/session-title-projection-flush/report.md`.
+
+## Release procedure
+
+This package is retired exam material: **do not publish it**. The earlier
+procedure (`pnpm publish --force`, "the registry checks are just warnings") must
+not be followed; `private: true` stays, and any version bump exists only to
+record the source change.
